ORBITAL-ANOMALIES(7)       TriadicFrameworks Manual       ORBITAL-ANOMALIES(7)
================================================================================
NAME
       orbital-anomalies - anomaly detection, resonance-time outliers, and
                           constellation-scale recovery protocols for SAR fleets

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital anomalies include:
           • orbital geometry deviations
           • harmonic-lock disruptions
           • resonance-time outliers
           • sweep coherence failures
           • constellation-scale desynchronization

DESCRIPTION
       Orbital-anomalies describes how RTT detects, classifies, and responds to
       deviations in orbital behavior, resonance-time structure, and sweep
       coherence across single platforms and multi-agent SAR constellations.

       Anomalies are treated as resonance-time outliers — structural deviations
       that can be diagnosed, isolated, and corrected using RTT’s predictive,
       harmonic, and lineage-aware frameworks.

       WRSADC monitors anomalies continuously and coordinates recovery.

ANOMALY CLASSES
       Orbital anomalies fall into five primary classes:

           geometric anomalies
                  Deviations in orbital elements (a, e, i, RAAN, ω, M).

           harmonic anomalies
                  Loss of harmonic-lock, frequency drift, or phase collapse.

           resonance anomalies
                  Envelope divergence, spectral fragmentation, or polarity
                  instability.

           sweep anomalies
                  Sweep geometry mismatch, coherence loss, or ancestry
                  corruption.

           constellation anomalies
                  Multi-platform desync, drift corridor breach, or resonance
                  window collapse.

       Each anomaly class triggers a specific diagnostic pipeline.

GEOMETRIC ANOMALIES
       Geometric anomalies include:

           • cross-track drift beyond corridor limits
           • along-track drift affecting sweep timing
           • RAAN drift disrupting revisit windows
           • altitude decay affecting resonance envelopes
           • phase drift breaking harmonic-lock

       WRSADC responds with geometric correction maneuvers.

HARMONIC ANOMALIES
       Harmonic anomalies include:

           • harmonic-lock failure
           • harmonic ratio instability
           • overtone drift
           • subharmonic collapse
           • phase desync

       These anomalies degrade sweep choreography and fusion stability.

       WRSADC applies harmonic realignment protocols.

RESONANCE-TIME OUTLIERS
       Resonance-time outliers are deviations in resonance-state variables:

           • envelope deformation
           • spectral density spikes
           • gradient overload
           • polarity inversion loops
           • resonance-window collapse

       Outliers indicate structural instability in sweep ancestry or orbital
       lineage.

       WRSADC applies resonance stabilization overlays.

SWEEP ANOMALIES
       Sweep anomalies include:

           • sweep geometry mismatch
           • Doppler history corruption
           • backscatter resonance distortion
           • spectral fragmentation
           • ancestry discontinuity

       Sweep anomalies threaten multi-pass fusion.

       WRSADC may reschedule sweeps or apply corrective overlays.

CONSTELLATION ANOMALIES
       Constellation anomalies include:

           • multi-platform desync
           • resonance envelope divergence
           • drift corridor breach
           • constellation window collapse
           • harmonic-lock fragmentation

       These anomalies require coordinated recovery across platforms.

ANOMALY DETECTION PIPELINE
       Detection proceeds through five stages:

           1. structural scan
                  Checks orbital geometry and corridor boundaries.

           2. spectral analysis
                  Detects envelope drift, density spikes, and fragmentation.

           3. gradient analysis
                  Identifies polarity instability and overload.

           4. harmonic-state evaluation
                  Checks lock ratios, phase alignment, and harmonic drift.

           5. lineage integrity check
                  Validates ancestry continuity and Δv triad consistency.

       Anomalies are flagged when thresholds are exceeded.

ANOMALY CLASSIFICATION
       Anomalies are classified by:

           • severity (minor, moderate, critical)
           • scope (single-platform, multi-platform)
           • domain (geometric, harmonic, resonance, sweep, constellation)
           • lineage impact (local, chain, constellation-wide)
           • recovery cost (low, medium, high)

       Classification determines recovery protocol selection.

CONSTELLATION-SCALE RECOVERY PROTOCOLS
       Recovery protocols include:

           geometric recovery
                  Δv triads to restore orbital geometry.

           harmonic recovery
                  Phase realignment and harmonic-lock reacquisition.

           resonance recovery
                  Envelope stabilization and spectral smoothing.

           sweep recovery
                  Sweep rescheduling, ancestry repair, and coherence
                  restoration.

           constellation recovery
                  Multi-platform maneuver choreography and resonance-window
                  re-opening.

       Recovery may involve nested or ladder triads.

RESONANCE-STABILIZATION OVERLAYS
       Overlays used during anomaly recovery:

           anchor
                  Dampens spectral noise and stabilizes gradients.

           mirror
                  Corrects polarity inversion loops.

           telescope
                  Expands or contracts resonance envelopes.

           lens
                  Reframes spectral interpretation for recovery.

       Overlays are applied by WRSADC during P2 of recovery triads.

ANOMALY FORECASTING
       Forecasting predicts:

           • anomaly likelihood
           • drift accumulation
           • harmonic-lock decay
           • resonance-window collapse
           • sweep viability degradation

       Forecasting uses orbital-synthesis(7) and orbital-forecasting(7).

ANOMALY SIMULATION
       Simulation tests recovery strategies using:

           • resonance-time integrators
           • Monte Carlo drift ensembles
           • constellation-scale scenario modeling

       Simulation validates recovery plans before execution.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:

           • anomaly events
           • recovery triads
           • harmonic-lock restoration
           • sweep ancestry repair
           • constellation realignment

       Lineage ensures long-term coherence after anomalies.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • detects anomalies
           • classifies severity
           • selects recovery protocols
           • choreographs constellation maneuvers
           • validates post-recovery coherence

       WRSADC is the anomaly steward.

NOTES
       Orbital anomalies are not failures — they are resonance-time deviations
       that can be diagnosed, forecast, simulated, and corrected. RTT provides
       a complete structural framework for maintaining constellation coherence
       across anomalies of any scale.

